00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.240
- So as most people are aware,

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.400
gaining access to
mainland Chinese archives

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.740
has gotten progressively more
difficult in recent years,

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:36.930
and COVID-19 has only
exacerbated the situation.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:40.240
In last summer's edition
of this webinar series,

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:42.270
two of the three of our webinars

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.170
addressed the challenge of
accessing Chinese archives.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:47.680
One of the webinars was on

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.650
introducing digital sources
for Chinese history,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.070
and one was on introducing collections

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:56.110
beyond the traditional Chinese archive.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:58.050
So they included collections
in the United States,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.750
in Taiwan, in Japan

00:01:59.750 --> 00:02:02.320
and on so-called garbology materials

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.440
or materials that are found
in unconventional places.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.903
We will drop the links for
those videos in the chat.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.270
So based on the responses
to last year's series,

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:16.340
we decided to dedicate a
full webinar this summer

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.010
