01:05:55.630 --> 01:05:56.463
Okay.

01:05:57.500 --> 01:05:59.070
I was born and raised in Hong Kong.

01:05:59.070 --> 01:06:01.430
So Taiwan or the Republic of China

01:06:01.430 --> 01:06:03.513
has never been unfamiliar for me,

01:06:04.570 --> 01:06:06.100
nor do I see the Republic of China

01:06:06.100 --> 01:06:08.100
as something in past tense.

01:06:08.100 --> 01:06:10.950
So the Republic is still ongoing,

01:06:10.950 --> 01:06:13.550
but back then I did not know
how relevant this could be

01:06:13.550 --> 01:06:15.223
to my own research and career.

01:06:18.200 --> 01:06:21.190
I did my masters degree on late
imperial history in Taiwan,

01:06:21.190 --> 01:06:22.680
on (speaks Chinese) history.

01:06:22.680 --> 01:06:25.160
I was attracted by the works
of the very strong cohort

01:06:25.160 --> 01:06:27.460
of late Imperial scholar (speaks Chinese).

01:06:27.460 --> 01:06:30.240
Here or actual (speaks
Chinese) is definitely

01:06:30.240 --> 01:06:33.430
one of the towering figures in this field.

01:06:33.430 --> 01:06:35.553
Only after I started my PhD in the States,

01:06:36.420 --> 01:06:40.180
I started to think seriously
about the history of

01:06:40.180 --> 01:06:43.113
the Republic of China
in Taiwan after 1949.

01:06:44.195 --> 01:06:46.620
As well I entered the modern China field

01:06:46.620 --> 01:06:50.740
and worked with Rebecca
Nedostup, my advisor,

01:06:50.740 --> 01:06:53.370
and she had already started
her fascinating project

01:06:53.370 --> 01:06:56.630
on displacement of both living
people and also dead bodies

01:06:56.630 --> 01:06:59.013
in mid-20th century China and Taiwan.

01:07:00.800 --> 01:07:05.163
We read documents together and
I benefited a lot from her.

01:07:06.420 --> 01:07:08.170
Over the course of my PhD research,

01:07:08.170 --> 01:07:10.750
I did archival and
library research in China,

01:07:10.750 --> 01:07:15.110
mostly the (speaks Chinese)
Second Historical Archive,

01:07:15.110 --> 01:07:17.330
library research in the US.

01:07:17.330 --> 01:07:20.130
I rely heavily on oral directs,

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:22.907
so I can stay in Prague
and then just go to Walcats

01:07:22.907 --> 01:07:26.460
and then order the books and
took the send to our library,

01:07:26.460 --> 01:07:28.911
which is not particularly,

01:07:28.911 --> 01:07:33.630
they don't have a very rich
collection of east Asian books,

01:07:33.630 --> 01:07:35.093
and also alas Taiwan.

01:07:36.191 --> 01:07:37.880
But when I started
writing my dissertation,

01:07:37.880 --> 01:07:40.420
I found that the materials
in Taiwan did not just

01:07:40.420 --> 01:07:44.180
support existing research agendas.

01:07:44.180 --> 01:07:47.650
They do not support
existing research questions.

01:07:47.650 --> 01:07:49.890
These materials in Taiwan also changed

01:07:49.890 --> 01:07:53.160
how I formulate my research questions,

01:07:53.160 --> 01:07:55.220
how I defined a scope of research,

01:07:55.220 --> 01:07:58.090
and actually quite
fundamentally has changed

01:07:58.090 --> 01:08:01.020
how I prioritize the 20th century

01:08:01.020 --> 01:08:02.713
Chinese and Taiwanese history.

01:08:03.660 --> 01:08:05.573
So move on.

01:08:07.000 --> 01:08:09.630
The question I came across
was how to make sense

01:08:09.630 --> 01:08:12.073
of the Republic of China beyond 1949.

01:08:13.090 --> 01:08:15.440
Who think the Republic of China after '49,

01:08:15.440 --> 01:08:17.713
the equation can change a lot.

01:08:19.000 --> 01:08:20.410
The question we asked for now

01:08:20.410 --> 01:08:22.340
will be confined to something like

01:08:22.340 --> 01:08:24.890
the cause of the 1949 tobacco,

01:08:24.890 --> 01:08:27.229
or how the wars supported the development

01:08:27.229 --> 01:08:30.103
during the Nanjing decade.

01:08:31.350 --> 01:08:32.850
We can now ask questions like,

01:08:32.850 --> 01:08:35.980
how did the ROC, the Republic of China,

01:08:35.980 --> 01:08:37.900
government and with the legal,

01:08:37.900 --> 01:08:42.120
which that function though with
a lot of flaws and problems

01:08:42.120 --> 01:08:45.000
for at least another
three decades in Taiwan,

01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:46.103
despite the wars.

01:08:47.430 --> 01:08:48.290
The wars in fact,

01:08:48.290 --> 01:08:51.290
helped to build and
consolidate the system.

01:08:51.290 --> 01:08:54.240
So we can ask very
different kinds of questions

01:08:54.240 --> 01:08:55.720
and we had to have very different kinds

01:08:55.720 --> 01:08:57.083
of prioritization scheme.

01:08:58.610 --> 01:09:01.550
Sometimes I would call this
"The Long Republican Period".

01:09:01.550 --> 01:09:03.220
At last, at the end,

01:09:03.220 --> 01:09:06.340
I wrote an dissertation that
spanned from 1905 to 1980s.

01:09:09.368 --> 01:09:13.894
Nearly one-third of this
is about post 1949 Taiwan.

01:09:13.894 --> 01:09:14.727
From then on,

01:09:14.727 --> 01:09:17.287
I did not just do Chinese
history in Taiwan,

01:09:17.287 --> 01:09:19.330
(speaks Chinese) presence in Taiwan,

01:09:19.330 --> 01:09:22.083
but I also do Chinese history in Taiwan,

01:09:23.320 --> 01:09:26.870
the Chinese history of the
Republic of China in Taiwan.

01:09:26.870 --> 01:09:29.250
That's why I choose this as the

01:09:29.250 --> 01:09:30.800
title of my presentation today.

01:09:32.107 --> 01:09:35.610
For archival research, we
have Feng-yuan and Ya-Hung,

01:09:35.610 --> 01:09:38.230
who have already done
a lot of heavy lifting,

01:09:38.230 --> 01:09:41.983
